G502:
*† ἄντλημα, -τος, τό (< ἀντλέω),
(a) prop., what is drawn (Diosc.);
(b) a vessel to draw with, a bucket (Plut.; v. Abbott, Essays, 88): John 4:11.†

G507:
ἄνω, adv. (< ἀνά),
(a) up, upwards: John 11:41, Hebrews 12:15;
(b) above (opp. to κάτω): Acts 2:19; with art. ἡ, Galatians 4:26, Philippians 3:14; τά, John 8:23, Colossians 3:1, Colossians 3:2; ἕως ἄ. (up to the brim), John 2:7 (Cremer, 106; MM, VGT, s.v.).†

G511:
ἀνώτερος, α, -ον, [in LXX: Nehemiah 3:25 (עֶלְיוֹן), Ezekiel 41:7 (מַעַל), To 8:3*;] only in neut., as adv. (cf. ἐξώτερος),
(a) of motion, higher: Luke 14:10;
(b) of rest, above, before: Hebrews 10:8.†

G514:
ἄξιος, -α, -ον (< ἄγω, in sense, to weigh), [in LXX for בֵּן (Deuteronomy 25:2), מָלֵא, שָׁוָה; freq. in Wis, II Mac;]
(a) of weight, worth (often c. gen., cf. Proverbs 3:15 Proverbs 8:11), seq. πρός: Romans 8:18 (v. Field, Notes, 157);
(b) befitting, meet: c. gen., Matthew 3:8, Luke 3:8 Luke 23:41, Acts 26:20, 1 Corinthians 16:4 (v. M, Pr., 216); absol., 2 Thessalonians 1:3;
(c) of persons, worthy;
α in good sense: c. gen. rei, Matthew 10:10, Luke 7:4 Luke 10:7, Acts 13:46, 1 Timothy 1:15 1 Timothy 4:9 1 Timothy 5:18 1 Timothy 6:1; c. aor. inf. (v. M, Pr., 203): Luke 15:19, Luke 15:21, Acts 13:25, Revelation 4:11 Revelation 5:2, Revelation 5:4, Revelation 5:9, Revelation 5:12; seq. ἵνα: John 1:27; ὅς, Luke 7:4; absol., but of what understood: Matthew 10:11, Matthew 10:13 Matthew 22:8, Revelation 3:4; c. gen. pers., Matthew 10:37, Matthew 10:38, Hebrews 11:38;
β in bad sense; c. gen. rei, Luke 12:48 Luke 23:15, Acts 23:29 Acts 25:11, Acts 25:25 Acts 26:31, Romans 1:32; absol., Revelation 16:6 (MM, VGT, s.v.).†

G515:
ἀξιόω, -ῶ (< ἄξιος), [in LXX chiefly for בּעא, בּקשׁ; freq. in Wi, I, II Mac;]
(a) to deem worthy: c. acc. et inf. (v. MM, VGT, s.v., and cf. κατ-αξιόω), Luke 7:7; id. et gen. rei, 2 Thessalonians 1:11; pass. c. gen. rei, 1 Timothy 5:17, Hebrews 3:3 Hebrews 10:29;
(b) to think fit: c. inf. (v. M, Pr., 205), Acts 15:38 Acts 28:22.†

G530:
ἅπαξ, adv., [in LXX for אֶחָד, פַּעַם;]
(a) once: 2 Corinthians 11:25, Hebrews 9:26, Hebrews 9:27; ἔτι ἅ., Hebrews 12:26, Hebrews 12:27; ἅ. τ. ἐνιαυτοῦ, Hebrews 9:7; καί ἅ. κ. δίς, twice: Philippians 4:16, 1 Thessalonians 2:18;
(b) once for all: Hebrews 6:4 Hebrews 9:28 Hebrews 10:2, 1 Peter 3:18, Jude 1:3, Jude 1:5 (MM, VGT, s.v.).†

G540:
* ἀπάτωρ, -ορος, ὁ, ὴ (< ἀ- neg., πατήρ);
1. fatherless.
2. without father (MM, VGT, s.v.), i.e., with no recorded genealogy: Hebrews 7:3.†

G565:
ἀπ-έρχομαι, [in LXX chiefly for הלךְ;]
1. to go away, depart (also, in late writers, with "perfective" force, to arrive at a destination, the thought being carried on to the goal; M, Pr., 111 f., 247; MM, s.v.);
(a) absol.: Matthew 13:25, al.; ptcp., ἀπελθών, used pleonastically with other verbs as in Heb. (Dalman, Words, 21), Matthew 13:28, al.;
(b) with mention of place or person: εἰς, Matthew 14:15; ἐπί, Luke 24:24; πρός, Revelation 10:9; ἀπό, Luke 1:38; ἐξω, Acts 4:15; ἐκεῖ, Matthew 2:22.
2. As in LXX, seq. ὀπίσω, c. gen. (Heb. הָלַךְ אַחֲרֵי), to go after, follow: Mark 1:20, John 12:19; metaph., Mark 1:42, Revelation 21:1.

G571:
ἄ-πιστος, -ον (< ἀ- neg., πιστός), [in LXX: Proverbs 17:6 Proverbs 28:25, Isaiah 17:10*;]
(a) of things, incredible: Acts 26:8;
(b) of persons, without faith or trust, unbelieving: Matthew 17:17, Mark 9:19, Luke 9:41 Luke 12:46, John 20:27, Titus 1:15, Revelation 21:8; specif., of unbelievers as opp. to Christians: 1 Corinthians 6:6 1 Corinthians 7:12-15 1 Corinthians 10:27 1 Corinthians 14:22-24, 2 Corinthians 4:4 2 Corinthians 6:14, 2 Corinthians 6:15, 1 Timothy 5:8 (cf. Lft., Notes, 265; Cremer, 491).†

G575:
ἀπό (on the freq. neglect of elision bef. vowels, v. Tdf., Pr., 94, WH, App., 146), prep. c. gen. (WM, 462 ff.; on its relation to ἐκ, παρά, ὑπό, WM, 456 f.), [in LXX for מִן, בְּ, לְ;] from (i.e. from the exterior).
1. Of separation and cessation;
(1) of motion from a place: Matthew 5:29, Matthew 5:30 Matthew 7:23, Luke 5:2 Luke 22:41, al.;
(2) in partitive sense (M, Pr., 72, 102, 245; MM, s.v.; Bl., § 40, 2), Matthew 9:16 Matthew 27:21, John 21:10, Acts 5:2, al.; also after verbs of eating, etc.;
(3) of alienation (cl. gen. of separation), after such verbs as λούω (Deiss., BS, 227), λύω, σώζω, παύω, etc.; ἀνάθεμα ἀ., Romans 9:3; ἀποθνήσκειν ἀ., Colossians 2:20; σαλευθῆναι, 2 Thessalonians 2:2, καθαρός, -ίζειν, ἀ. (Deiss., BS, 196, 216), Acts 20:26, 2 Corinthians 7:1, Hebrews 9:14;
(4) of position, Matthew 23:34 Matthew 24:31, al.; after μακράν, Matthew 8:30; transposed before measures of distance, John 10:18 John 21:8, Revelation 14:20 (Abbott, JG, 227);
(5) of time, ἀπὸ τ. ὥρας, ἡμέρας, etc., Matthew 9:22, John 19:27, Acts 20:18, Philippians 1:5, al.; ἀπ’ αἰῶνος, Luke 1:70, al.; ἀπ’ ἀρχῆς, etc., Matthew 19:4, Romans 1:20; ἀπὸ βρέφους, 2 Timothy 3:15; ἀφ’ ἧς, since, Luke 7:45, al.; ἀπὸ τ. νῦν, Luke 1:48, al.; ἀπὸ τότε, Matthew 4:17, al.; ἀπὸ πέρυσι, a year ago, 2 Corinthians 8:10 2 Corinthians 9:2; ἀπὸ πρωΐ, Acts 28:23;
(6) of order or rank, ἀπὸ διετοῦς, Matthew 2:16; ἀπὸ Ἀβραάμ, Matthew 1:17; ἕβδομος ἀπὸ Ἀδάμ, Jude 1:14; ἀπὸ μικροῦ ἕως μεγάλου, Acts 8:10, Hebrews 8:11; ἄρχεσθαι ἀπό, Matthew 20:8, John 8:9, Acts 8:35, al.
2. Of origin;
(1) of birth, extraction, and hence, in late writers,
(a) of local extraction (cl. ἐξ; Abbott, JG, 227 ff.), Matthew 21:11, Mark 15:43, John 1:45, Acts 10:38, al.; οἱ ἀπὸ Ἰταλίας (WM, § 66, 6; M, Pr., 237; Westc, Rendall, in l.), Hebrews 13:24;
(b) of membership in a community or society (Bl., § 40, 2), Acts 12:1, al.;
(c) of material (= cl. gen.; Bl., § 40, 2; M, Pr., 102), Matthew 3:4 Matthew 27:21;
(d) after verbs of asking, seeking, etc., Luke 11:50, Luke 11:51, 1 Thessalonians 2:6 (Milligan, in l.);
(2) of the cause, instrument, means or occasion (freq. = ὑπό, παρά, and after verbs of learning, hearing, knowing, etc.; Bl., § 40, 3), Matthew 7:16 Matthew 11:29, Luke 22:45, Acts 2:22 Acts 4:36 Acts 9:13 Acts 12:14, 1 Corinthians 11:23, Galatians 3:2, al.; ἀπὸ τ. ὄχλου, Luke 19:3 (cf. John 21:6, Acts 22:11); ἀπὸ τ. φόβου, Matthew 14:26, al. (cf. Matthew 10:28 Matthew 13:44).
3. Noteworthy Hellenistic phrases: φοβεῖσθαι ἀπό (M, Pr., 102, 107); προσέχειν ἀπό (M, Pr., 102, 107; Milligan, NTD, 50); ἀπὸ νότου (Heb. מִנֶּגֶב), Revelation 21:13; ἀπὸ προσώπου (מִפְּנֵי), 2 Thessalonians 1:9 (Bl., § 40, 9); ἀπὸ τ. καρδιῶν (בְּלֵב), Matthew 18:35; ἀπὸ ὁ ὤν (WM, § 10, 2; M, Pr., 9), Revelation 1:4.
4. In composition, ἀπό denotes separation, departure, origin, etc. (ἀπολύω, ἀπέρχομαι, ἀπογράφω); it also has a perfective force (M, Pr., 112, 247), as in ἀφικνεῖσθαι, ἀφικνεῖσθαι, q.v.

G580:
* ἀπο-βολή, -ῆς, ἡ (< ἀποβάλλω);
1. a throwing away, rejection: opp. to πρόσλημψις, Romans 11:15.
2. a losing, loss: Acts 27:22.†

G610:
*† ἀπό-κριμα, -τος, τό (< ἀποκρίνω);
1. prop., a judicial sentence: 2 Corinthians 1:9, R, mg.
2. an answer (v. Thayer, s.v.): 2 Corinthians 1:9, R, txt. (In FlJ, Ant., xiv, 10, 6, of a rescript of the Senate; in Inscr. of an official decision, Deiss., BS, 257; a reply to a deputation, MM, s.v.: cf. also Cremer, 375).†

G622:
ἀπ-όλλυμι and ἀπολλύω, [in LXX for אבד, etc. (38 words in all)].
1. Act.,
(1) to destroy utterly, destroy, kill: Mark 1:24, Mark 9:22, al.; τ. ψνχήν, Matthew 10:28, al.;
(2) to lose utterly: Matthew 10:42, al.; metaph., of failing to save, John 6:39 John 18:9.
2. Mid.,
(1) to perish;
(a) of things: Matthew 5:29, John 6:12, Hebrews 1:11(LXX), al.;
(b) of persons: Matthew 8:26, al. Metaph., of loss of eternal life, John 3:15, John 3:16 John 10:28 John 17:12, Romans 2:12, 1 Corinthians 8:11 1 Corinthians 15:18, 2 Peter 3:9. In οἱ ἀπολλύμενοι, the perishing, contrasted in 1 Corinthians 1:18, al., with οἱ σωζόμενοι, the "perfective" force of the verb, wh. "implies the completion of the process of destruction," is illustrated (v. M, Pr., 114 f.; M, Th., ii, 2:10);
(2) to be lost: Luke 15:4 Luke 21:18. Metaph., on the basis of the relation between shepherd and flock, of spiritual destitution and alienation from God: Matthew 10:6 Matthew 15:24, Luke 19:10 (MM, s.v.; DCG, i, 191 f., ii, 76, 554; Cremer, 451).

G649:
ἀπο-στέλλω, [in LXX very freq., almost always for שׁלח;] prop., to send away, to dispatch on service;
1. to send with a commission, or on service;
(a) of persons: Christ, Matthew 10:40; the apostles, Matthew 10:16; servants, Mark 12:2; angels, Mark 13:27;
(b) of things: ὄνος, Matthew 21:3; τὸ δρέπανον, Mark 4:29; τ. λόγον, Acts 10:36; τ. ἐπαγγελίαν (i.e. the promised Holy Spirit), Luke 24:49, Rec.; seq. εἰς, Matthew 20:2, Luke 11:49, John 3:17; ὀπίσω, Luke 19:14; ἔμπροσθεν, John 3:28; πρὸ προσώπου, Matthew 11:10; πρός, Matthew 21:34; with ref. to sender or place of departure: ἀπό, Luke 1:26 (Rec. ὑπό); παρά, John 1:6; ἐκ, John 1:19; ὑπό, Acts 10:17 (Rec. ἀπό); seq. inf., Mark 3:14, al.; ἵνα, Mark 12:2, al.; εἰς (of purpose), Hebrews 1:14; without direct obj.: seq. πρός, John 5:33; λέγων, John 11:3; ἀποστείλας, c. indic., Matthew 2:16, Acts 7:14, Revelation 1:1.
2. to send away, dismiss: Luke 4:18, Mark 5:10 Mark 8:26 Mark 12:3 (cf. ἐξ-, συν-αποστέλλω). SYN.: πέμπω, the general term. ἀ. "suggests official or authoritative sending" (v. Thayer, s.v. πέμπω; Westc., Jo., 298; Epp. Jo., 125; Cremer, 529; MM, s.v.).

G654:
ἀπο-στρέφω, [in LXX chiefly for שׁוּב;] trans., c. acc.,
(a) to turn away, remove: Romans 11:26, 2 Timothy 4:4; metaph., to turn away from allegiance, pervert: Luke 23:14;
(b) to turn back, return: μάχαιραν, Matthew 26:52. Pass., reflex., to turn oneself away from: c. acc., Matthew 5:42, 2 Timothy 1:15, Titus 1:14, Hebrews 12:25; so act., absol., Acts 3:26 (cf. Si 8:5; Bl., § 53, 1; Cremer, 880).†

G657:
ἀπο-τάσσω, [in LXX: Ecclesiastes 2:20 (יאשׁ pi.), I Es 6:27, Jeremiah 20:2, I Mac4*;] to set apart. Mid., in late Gk. (Bl., § 37, 1; Swete, Mk., 136 f.; MM, s.v.), c. dat.,
(a) to take leave of: Mark 6:46, Luke 9:61, Acts 18:18, Acts 18:21, 2 Corinthians 2:13;
(b) to forsake: Luke 14:33.†

G659:
ἀπο-τίθημι, [in LXX chiefly for נוּחַ hi.;] to put off or aside; in NT always mid.,
(a) to put off from oneself as a garment: τ. ἱμάτια, Acts 7:58; metaph., in ethical sense, to put of, lay aside: Romans 13:12, Ephesians 4:22, Ephesians 4:25, Colossians 3:8, Hebrews 12:1, James 1:21, 1 Peter 2:1;
(b) to stow away, put: ἐν τ. φυλακῇ (MM, s.v.), Matthew 14:3.†

G666:
* ἀπουσία, -ας, ἡ (< ἄπειμι);
1. absence (Æsch., Thuc.): Philippians 2:12.
2. deficiency, waste (MM, s.v).†

G694:
ἀργύριον, -ου, τό (< ἄργυρος), [in LXX for כֶּסֶף (Genesis 13:2, al.), exc. Lamentations 4:1 (כֶּתֶם);] prop., a piece of silver (Lft., Notes, 191); in NT,
(a) silver: Acts 3:6 Acts 7:16 Acts 19:19 Acts 20:33, 1 Corinthians 3:12, 1 Peter 1:18;
(b) money: Matthew 25:18, Matthew 25:27, Mark 14:11, Luke 9:3 Luke 19:15, Luke 19:23 Luke 22:5, Acts 8:20;
(c) a silver coin: pl., Matthew 26:15 Matthew 27:3, Matthew 27:5, Matthew 27:6, Matthew 27:9 Matthew 28:12, Matthew 28:15, where the value is that of a shekel or tetradrachm; ἀργυρίου μυριάδες πέντε (prob. drachmas; MM, s.v.), Acts 19:19.†

G703:
ἀρετή, -ῆς, ἡ, [in LXX, in sing.: Habakkuk 3:3, Zechariah 6:13 (הוֹד), in pl.: Isaiah 42:8, Isaiah 42:12 Isaiah 43:21 Isaiah 63:7 (תְּהִלָּה), Esther 14:10, Wi 4:1 5:13 8:7, II-IV Mac22*;] prop., whatever procures pre-eminent estimation for a person or thing, in Hom. any kind of conspicuous advantage. Later confined by philos. writers to intrinsic eminence—moral goodness, virtue;
(a) of God: 2 Peter 1:3;
(b) of men: Philippians 4:8, 2 Peter 1:5; pl. (Isaiah 42:8, Isaiah 42:12 Isaiah 43:21 Isaiah 63:7, Esther 14:10), excellencies: 1 Peter 2:9 (the usage appears to be a survival of an early comprehensive sense in which the original idea is blended with the impression which it makes on others, i.e. praise, renown; v. Hort, I Pe., 129. Deiss., BS, 95 f., thinks it means manifestations of divine power, as in current Gk. speech; cf. also MM, s.v.)†

G725:
*† ἁρπαγμός, οῦ, ὁ (< ἁρπάζω);
1. prop., acc., to the rule of its formation (Bl., § 27, 2), actively, the act of seizing, robbery (Plut., de Puer. Educ., p. 12A), Philippians 2:6, AV (Waterland, Works, II, 108; Cremer, 649 f.; Meyer, in l.; cf. also JThS, July, 1909, April, 1911; MM, s.v.).
2. Passively = ἅρπαγμα (Ezekiel 22:25, of a lion's prey, טֶרֶף), a thing seized, hence, a prize: Philippians 2:6, RV (Lft., Ellic., ICC, in l.; Donaldson, NCrat., 450 ff.; and esp. Gifford, The Incarnation, 59-71, and reff. in DB, ii, 835 B). The lexical data favour the active meaning, but as they also admit the possibility of the alternative, most modern expositors have accepted the latter as seeming to suit the logic of the passage better. The lexical difficulty, however, remains (MM, s.v., esp. the last ref.). As to the usage of St. Paul, he seems inclined to adopt the -μα form where it is appropriate (e.g. Romans 11:9, where cf. LXX; 1 Corinthians 13:9, 2 Corinthians 1:9), and there is certainly a presumption in favour of the active meaning here from the fact that he does not use the LXX ἅρπαγμα. Suggestions looking to a fresh exegesis are given in JThS, July, 1909, April, 1911.†

G746:
ἀρχή, -ῆς, ἡ, [in LXX for קֶדֶם, רֹאשׁ, רֵאשִׁית, etc.;]
1. beginning, origin;
(a) absol., of the beginning of all things: of God as the Eternal, the First Cause, Revelation 21:6 (cf. Revelation 1:8); similarly, of Christ, Revelation 22:13; of Christ as the uncreated principle, the active cause of creation, Revelation 3:14; in his relation to the Church, Colossians 1:18; ἐν ἀ., John 1:1, John 1:2; ἀπ᾽ ἀ. (and ἀπ᾽ ἀ. κτισεως), Matthew 19:4, Matthew 19:8 Matthew 24:21, Mark 10:6 Mark 13:19, John 8:44, 2 Thessalonians 2:13, 2 Peter 3:4, 1 John 1:1 1 John 2:13, 1 John 2:14 1 John 3:8; κατ’ ἀρχάς, Hebrews 1:10;
(b) relatively: Hebrews 7:3; ἀ. ὠδίνων, Matthew 24:8, Mark 13:9; τ. σημείων, John 2:11; τ. ὑποστάσεως, Hebrews 3:14; τ. λογίων, Hebrews 5:12; ὁ τ. ἀρχῆς τ. Χριστοῦ λόγος, the account of the beginning, the elementary view of Christ, Hebrews 6:1; ἀρχὴν λαμβάνειν, to begin, Hebrews 2:3; ἐξ ἀ., John 6:64 John 16:4; ἀπ’ ἀ., Luke 1:2, John 15:27, 1 John 2:7, 1 John 2:24 1 John 3:11, 2 John 1:5, 2 John 1:6; ἐν ἀ., Acts 11:15 Acts 26:4, Philippians 4:15; τὴν ἀρχήν, adverbially, at all (Hdt., al.; v MM, s.v.): John 8:25.
2. an extremity, a corner: Acts 10:11 Acts 11:5.
3. sovereignty, principality, rule (cf. DB, i, 616 f.): Luke 12:11 Luke 20:20, Romans 8:38, 1 Corinthians 15:24, Ephesians 1:21 Ephesians 3:10 Ephesians 6:12, Colossians 1:16 Colossians 2:10, Colossians 2:15, Titus 3:1, Jude 1:6 (Cremer, 113).†

G747:
ἀρχηγός, -όν, [in LXX for רֹאשׁ, נָשִׂיא etc.;] beginning, originating: more freq., as subst.;
1. founder, author (Lat. auchor; so sometimes in π., v. MM, s.v.; Milligan, NTD, 75): Acts 3:15 (R, mg.), Hebrews 2:10 (R, txt.; but v. Westc., in l., and Page, Acts 3:15).
2. prince, leader (so in MGr., v. Kennedy, Sources, 153): Acts 3:15 (R, txt.) Acts 5:31, Hebrews 2:10 (cf. R, mg.) Hebrews 12:2 (Cremer, 117).†

G786:
* ἄσπονδος, -ον (< σπονδή, a libation);
1. without truce (Thuc.).
2. admitting of no truce, implacable (Dem., al.): 2 Timothy 3:3.†

G798:
ἄστρον, -ου, τό, [in LXX chiefly for כּוֹכָב;]
(a) mostly in pl. (as in cl.), the stars: Luke 21:25, Acts 27:20, Hebrews 11:12;
(b) in sing. (Xen., al.), only of some noted star; the symbol or image of a star, Acts 7:43 (cf. ἀστήρ, and v. MM, s.v.).†

G806:
ἀσφαλῶς, adv., [in LXX: Genesis 34:25 (בֶּטַח), To 6:4, Wi 18:6, Ba 5:7, I Mac 6:40, III Mac 7:6*;]
(a) safely: Mark 14:44, Acts 16:23;
(b) assuredly: Acts 2:36.†

G841:
* αὐτάρκεια, -ας, ἡ (< αὐτάρκης, q.v.),
(a) sufficiency (MM, s.v.): in subjective sense (v. Milligan, NTD, 57), 2 Corinthians 9:8;
(b) contentment: 1 Timothy 6:6.†

G846:
αὐτός, -ή, -ό, determinative pron., in late Gk. much more freq. than in cl. (WM, 178 f.; Jannaris, HGG, §1399).
1. Emphatic (so always in nom. exc. when preceded by the art., v. infr., iii);
(1) self (ipse), expressing opposition, distinction, exclusion, etc., αὐ. ἐκχυθήσεται, Luke 5:37; αὐ. ἐγινώσκεν, John 2:25; αὐ. ὑμεῖς, John 3:28; καὶ αὐ. ἐγώ, Romans 15:14; αὐ. Ἰησοῦς, John 2:24; αὐ. καὶ οἱ μετ’ αὐτοῦ, Matthew 2:25; ὑμεῖς αὐ., Mark 6:31; esp. (as freq in cl.) αὐ. ὁ, Matthew 3:4, Mark 6:17, John 16:27, 1 Thessalonians 3:11, al.; in late Gk., sometimes weakened, ἐν αὐτῇ τ. ὥρᾳ, in that hour, Luke 10:21 (M, Pr., 91; MM, s.v.);
(2) emphatic, he, she, it (M, Pr., 86; Bl., § 48, 1, 2, 7), Matthew 1:21 Matthew 12:50, Luke 6:35, al.; pointing to some one as master (cl.), Matthew 8:24, Mark 4:38, al.; αὐ., καὶ αὐ. = οὗτος, ὁ δε (Bl., § 48, 1), Matthew 14:2, Mark 14:15, Mark 14:44, Luke 1:22 Luke 2:28, al.
2. In oblique cases (cl.), for the simple pron. of 3rd pers., he, she, it, Matthew 7:9, Matthew 10:12, Matthew 26:44, al.; with ptcp. in gen. absol., Matthew 9:18, Mark 13:1, al. (for irreg. constructions, V. Bl., § 74, 5); pleonastically after the relative (cf. Heb. אֲשֶׁר לֹו; WM, 184 ff.; Bl., § 50, 4; MM, s.v.), Mark 7:25, Revelation 3:8, Revelation 7:2, al.; in constr. ad sensum, without proper subject expressly indicated, Matthew 4:23, Acts 8:5, 2 Corinthians 2:13, al.; gen. αὐτοῦ = ἐκείνου, Romans 11:11, 1 Thessalonians 2:19, Titus 3:5, Hebrews 2:4.
3. ὁ, ἡ, τὸ αὐ., the same: Hebrews 1:12, Hebrews 13:8; τὸ αὐ., ποιεῖν, Matthew 5:46, Matthew 5:47, al.; φρονεῖν, Romans 12:16 Romans 15:5, Philippians 2:2, al.; τὰ αὐ., Acts 15:27, Romans 2:1, al.; κατὰ τὸ (τὰ) αὐ. (MM, s.v.), Acts 14:1, Luke 6:23, al.; ἐπὶ τὸ αὐ., together (MM, s.v.), Matthew 22:34, Acts 1:15, al.; ἓν κ. τὸ αὐ., 1 Corinthians 11:5, 1 Corinthians 12:11; c. dat. (cl.), 1 Corinthians 11:5; with a noun, λόγος, Mk 14:39; μέτρος, Philippians 1:30; πνεῦμα, 1 Corinthians 12:4.

G850:
* αὐχμηρός, -ά, -όν (< αὐχμός, drought);
1. dry.
2. squalid, dismal: 2 Peter 1:19 (MM, s.v.).†

G857:
* ἀφειδία (L, -εία), -ας, ἡ (< ἀφειδής, unsparing);
1. extravagance.
2. unsparing treatment, severity: Colossians 2:23.†

G862:
** ἄ-φθαρτος, -ον (< φθείρω), [in LXX: Wi 12:1 18:4*;] imperishable, immortal;
(a) of things: 1 Corinthians 9:25, 1 Peter 1:4, 1 Peter 1:23 1 Peter 3:4;
(b) of persons: of men, 1 Corinthians 15:52; of God, Romans 1:23, 1 Timothy 1:17 (MM, s.v.).†

G872:
ἀφ-οράω, -ῶ, [in LXX: IV Mac 17:10 (είς θεόν)*;]
(a) to look away from all else at, fix one's gaze upon: metaph. (MM, s.v.), Hebrews 12:2;
(b) simply, to see: ἀφίδω (v.s. ἀπεῖδον, and Lft., Phl., in l.; MM, s.v.), Philippians 2:23 (v. Ellic., in l.).†

G873:
ἀφ-ορίζω, [in LXX (Cremer, 805 f.) for בּדל hi., סגר hi., נוף hi., רום hi etc.;]
(a) to mark off by boundaries from, separate from: c. acc., Acts 19:9, Galatians 2:12; id. seq. ἐκ (ἀπό), Matthew 13:49 Matthew 25:32 (MM, s.v.); of excommunication, Luke 6:22. Pass., absol., 2 Corinthians 6:17;
(b) to set apart, devote to a special purpose (seq. εἰς): c. acc., Galatians 1:15. Mid., Acts 13:2, Romans 1:1 (DB, iii 588).†

G884:
** ἀχάριστος, -ον (< χαρίζομαι), [in LXX: Wi 16:29, Si 29:17, 25, IV Mac 9:10*;]
(a) ungracious, unpleasing;
(b) ungrateful, thankless: Luke 6:35, 2 Timothy 3:2.†

G891:
ἄχρι (and Epic ἄχρις, bef. vowel (v. MM, s.v.), Romans 11:25 T, Galatians 3:19 T, WH, mg., Hebrews 3:13);
1. adv., utterly (Hom.).
2. Prep. c. gen., until, unto, as far as;
(a) of time: Acts 3:21 Acts 22:22, Romans 1:13 Romans 5:13, 1 Corinthians 4:11, 2 Corinthians 3:14, Galatians 4:2, Philippians 1:6; ἄ. καιροῦ, Luke 4:13, Acts 13:11; ἄ. ἧς ἡμέρας, Matthew 24:38, Luke 1:20 Luke 17:27, Acts 1:2 (τῆς ἡ. ἧς), Acts 1:22; ἄ. ταύτης τ. ἡμέρας (τ. ἡ. ταύτης), Acts 2:29 Acts 23:1 Acts 26:22; ἄ. ἡμερῶν πέντε, Acts 20:6; ἄ. αὐγῆς, Acts 20:11; ἄ. τοῦ νῦν, Romans 8:22, Philippians 1:5; ἄ. τέλους, Hebrews 6:11, Revelation 2:26;
(b) of space: Acts 11:5 Acts 13:6 Acts 20:4 (R, txt., WH, mg.) Acts 28:15, 2 Corinthians 10:13, 2 Corinthians 10:14, Hebrews 4:12, Revelation 14:20 Revelation 18:5;
(c) of measure or degree: ἄ. θανάτου, Acts 22:4, Revelation 2:10 Revelation 12:11.
3. As conj., until;
(a) ἄχρι alone: c. subj. aor., Galatians 3:19 (ἄ. οὗ T, WH, mg.); id. without ἄν (Bl., § 65, 10), Revelation 7:3 Revelation 15:8 Revelation 20:3, Revelation 20:5; c. indic. fut., Revelation 17:17;
(b) ἄ. οὗ (i.e. ἄ. τούτου ᾧ): c. indic. aor., Acts 7:18; impf., Acts 27:33; c. subj. aor. (Bl., ut supr.), Luke 21:24, Romans 11:25, 1 Corinthians 11:26 1 Corinthians 15:25, Galatians 3:19, T, WH, mg.; id. with ἄν, Revelation 2:25; c. indic. pres., while, Hebrews 3:13 (cf. μέχρι).†

G907:
βαπτίζω (< βάπτω), [in LXX: 2 Kings 5:14 (טבל), Isaiah 21:4, Jth 12:7, Si 31 (34):30*;] to dip, immerse, sink;
1. generally (in Polyb., iii, 72, of soldiers wading breast-deep; in i, 51, of the sinking of ships); metaph., to overwhelm (Isaiah 21:4; cf. MM, Exp., x); c. cogn. acc., βάπτισμα β., Mark 10:38, Mark 10:39, Luke 12:50. Mid.,
2. to perform ablutions, wash oneself, bathe (2 Kings 5:14, Jth 12:7, Si 31 (34):30): Mark 7:4; aor. pass, in same sense, Luke 11:38.
3 Of ablution, immersion, as a religious rite, to baptize;
(a) absol.; Mark 1:4, John 1:25, John 1:26, John 1:28 John 3:22, John 3:23, John 3:26 John 4:2 John 10:40, 1 Corinthians 1:17; ὁ βαπτίζων (= ὁ βαπτιστής, M, Pr., 127), Mark 6:14, Mark 6:24; c. acc., John 4:1, Acts 8:38, 1 Corinthians 1:14 1 Corinthians 1:14, 1 Corinthians 1:16; c. cogn. acc., τὸ βάπτισμα, Acts 19:4 (cf. Mark 10:38, supr.); pass., to be baptized, receive baptism: Matthew 3:13, Matthew 3:14, Matthew 3:16, Mark 16:16, Luke 3:7, Luke 3:12, Luke 3:21 Luke 7:29 (τ. βάπτισμα) Luke 7:30, Acts 2:41 Acts 8:12, Acts 8:13, Acts 8:36 Acts 9:18 Acts 10:47 Acts 16:15, Acts 16:33 Acts 18:8; mid., Acts 22:16 (M, Pr., 163);
(b) with prepositions: ἐν, of the element, Matthew 3:6, Matthew 3:11, Mark 1:4, Mark 1:5, Mark 1:8, Luke 3:16, John 1:26, John 1:31, John 1:33 John 3:23, Acts 1:5 Acts 2:38 Acts 10:48 Acts 11:16, 1 Corinthians 10:2; εἰς, of the element, purpose or result (Lft., Notes, 155), Matthew 3:11 Matthew 28:19, Mark 1:9, Acts 8:16 Acts 19:3, Acts 19:5, Romans 6:3, 1 Corinthians 1:13, 1 Corinthians 1:15 10:2 1 Corinthians 12:13, Galatians 3:27; c. dat., ὕδατι, Luke 3:16, Acts 1:5 Acts 11:16; ὑπὲρ τῶν νεκρῶν, perh. to fulfil the wish of a dead friend, 1 Corinthians 15:29 (v. ICC, in l.; cf. DB, i, 238 ff.; DCG, i, 169a; ii, 605b; Cremer, 126).†

G908:
*† βάπτισμα, -τος, τό (< βαπτίζω) , prop., the result of the act, τὸ βαπτίζειν, as distinct from βαπτισμός, the act itself, immersion, baptism;
1. metaph., of affliction: Mark 10:38, Mark 10:39, Luke 12:50.
2. Of the religious rite of baptism;
(a) of John's baptism: Matthew 3:7 Matthew 21:25, Mark 11:30, Luke 7:29 Luke 20:4, Acts 1:22 Acts 10:37 Acts 18:25 Acts 19:3; β. μετανοίας, Mark 1:4, Luke 3:3, Acts 13:24 Acts 19:4;
(b) of Christian baptism; Romans 6:4, Ephesians 4:5, Colossians 2:12 (Tr., -μῷ, q.v.), 1 Peter 3:21 (cf. Cremer, 130; Tr., Syn. § xcix).†

G909:
*† βαπτισμός, -οῦ, ὁ (< βαπτίζω) , prop., the act of which βάπτισμα is the result;
1. a dipping, washing, lustration: Mark 7:4; of Jewish ceremonial, Hebrews 9:10; in Hebrews 6:2, βαπτισμῶν διδαχήν (-ῆς), "the pl. and the peculiar form seem to be used to include Christian baptism with other lustral rites" (Westc, in l.).
2. baptism: FlJ, Ant., 18, 5, 2 (of John's baptism), and some Fathers (v. Soph., s.v.). Not so in NT, unless ἐν τ. βαπτισμῷ, in the act of baptism, Colossians 2:12, be read with Tr. (Rec., WH, R, -ματι).†

G911:
βάπτω, [in LXX chiefly for טבל;]
(a) to dip: Luke 16:24, John 13:26 (ἐμβ-, L);
(b) to dip in dye, to dye: Revelation 19:13 (Rec.; ῥεραντισμένον, WH; περιρεραμμένον, T; ῥεραμμένον, Swete, in l., q.v.).†

G922:
βάρος, -εος, τό, [in LXX: Judges 18:21 (כּבד), Jth 7:4, Si 13:2, II Mac 9:10, III Mac 5:47*;] weight;
(a) a weight, burden, lit. and metaph.: Matthew 20:12, Acts 15:28, 2 Corinthians 4:17, Revelation 2:24; ἀλλήλων τὰ β., one another's faults, Galatians 6:2; ἐν β., burdensome: 1 Thessalonians 2:6, R, txt., but v. infr.;
(b) in late Gk. (Soph., Lex., s.v.), dignity, authority: ἐν βάρει, 1 Thessalonians 2:6 (R, mg.; v. Milligan, ICG, in l.).† SYN.: ὄγκος, an encumbrance; φορτίον, a burden, that which is borne.

G963:
Βηθανία, -ας (also -ιά, indecl., Luke 19:29 and in B*, Mark 11:1), ἡ, (Heb. בֵּית עֲנִיָּה, house of affliction, acc. to Jerome, or perh., house of dates, cf. Bethphage), Bethany;
1. a village fifteen furlongs from Jerusalem, the modern El Azerîyeh: Matthew 21:17 Matthew 26:6, Mark 8:22 (WH, mg.) Mark 11:1, Mark 11:11, Mark 11:12 Mark 14:3, Luke 19:29 Luke 24:50, John 11:1, John 11:18 John 12:1.
2. A place on E. bank of Jordan: John 1:28 (R, mg., Βηθαβαρά, q.v.).†

G973:
*† βιαστής, -οῦ, ὁ (< βιάζω), late form of βιατάς;
1. strong, forceful.
2. violent (Philo): Matthew 11:12 (v.s. βιάζω).†

G988:
βλασφημία, -ας, ἡ (< βλάσφημος), [in LXX: Ezekiel 35:12 (נֶאָצָה), Daniel 3:29 (שׁלה), To 1:18, I Mac 2:6, II Mac 8:4 10:35 15:24 * ;]
(a) railing slander: Matthew 12:31 Matthew 15:19, Mark 3:28 Mark 7:22, Ephesians 4:31, Colossians 3:8, 1 Timothy 6:4, Jude 1:9, Revelation 2:9;
(b) spec., impious speech against God, blasphemy: Matthew 26:65, Mark 14:64, Luke 5:21, John 10:33, Revelation 13:5; ὄνομα βλασφημίας, Revelation 13:1 Revelation 17:3; c. gen. obj. Matthew 12:31; πρὸς τ. θεόν, Revelation 13:6 (Cremer, 570; DB, i, 305; DCG ii, 423).†

G989:
βλάσφημος, -ον (< βλασ-, of uncertain deriv., v. Thayer, Boisacq; + φήμη, speech), [in LXX: Isaiah 66:3 (מְבָרֵךְ אָוֶן), Wi 1:6, Si 3:16, II Mac 9:28 10:4, 36 * ;]
(a) evil-speaking, slanderous, blasphemous: Acts 6:11, 2 Timothy 3:2, 2 Peter 2:11 (cf. Jude 1:9);
(b) as subst. a blasphemer: 1 Timothy 1:13 (Cremer, 570).†

G991:
βλέπω, [in LXX chiefly for ראה, also for פּנה, etc. ;]
1. of bodily sight;
(a) to see, have sight (opp. to τυφλὸς εἶναι): Matthew 12:22, John 9:7, Acts 9:9, Romans 11:8, Revelation 3:18, al.;
(b) to perceive, look (at), see: absol., Acts 1:9; c. acc., Matthew 7:3, Mark 5:31, Luke 6:41, John 1:29, al.; ὅραμα, Acts 12:9; γυναῖκα, Matthew 5:28; βιβλίον, Revelation 5:3, Revelation 5:4; τ. βλεπόμενα, 2 Corinthians 4:18.
2. Metaph., of mental vision;
(a) to see, perceive, discern: absol., Matthew 13:13, Luke 8:10; δι’ ἐσόπτρου, 1 Corinthians 13:12; c. acc., Hebrews 2:9 Hebrews 10:25; seq. ὅτι, Hebrews 3:19, James 2:22;
(b) to consider, look to, take heed: absol., Mark 13:23, Mark 13:33; c. acc., 1 Corinthians 1:26, al.; seq. πῶς, c. indic., Luke 8:18, 1 Corinthians 3:10, Ephesians 5:15; seq. τί, c. indic., Mark 4:24; seq. εἰς πρόσωπον, of partiality, Matthew 22:16, Mark 12:14. Colloq. (for ex. from π., V. Deiss., LAE, 122; M, Pr., 107; MM, Exp., x; Milligan, NTD, 50), β. ἑαυτόν: Mark 13:9; seq. ἵνα μή, 2 John 1:8; β. ἀπό, Mark 8:15 Mark 12:38; seq. μή (cl. ὁρᾶν), c. fut. indic., Colossians 2:8, Hebrews 3:12; id. c. aor. subj., Matthew 24:4, Mark 13:5.
3. Of situation and direction (Lat. specto), to look, face (towards), places, etc. (seq. πρός, Xen, Hell., vii, 1, 17; Ezekiel 40:23, 24): seq. κατά, c. acc., Acts 27:12 (cf. ἀνα-, ἀπο-, δια-, ἐμ-, ἐπι-, περ-, προ-βλέπω), v. DCG, i, 446; ii, 596.
